 ("Comprehension: Main Idea & Supporting Details",
  [("Introduction", ["Comprehension is understanding what you read. The main idea is what the whole passage is about; supporting details are the facts, examples and reasons that develop it."]),
   ("Finding the main idea", ["Read the passage once quickly (skimming) for the gist. Look for the topic sentence — usually the first or last sentence of the paragraph. Ask: 'What is the writer saying about the topic?' Express it in one sentence: that is the main idea."]),
   ("Identifying details", ["Supporting details answer who, what, when, where, why and how. Distinguish facts (verifiable) from opinions (personal views). Inference goes beyond what is stated — what the writer suggests but does not say directly."]),
   ("Summary skills", ["State the main idea in your own words; join related details into one or two sentences; never copy the passage word for word; keep to the instruction (e.g. one sentence, four points)."]),
   ("Class evaluation", ["Read a short passage and state its main idea in one sentence.", "What is the difference between a fact and an opinion?", "Explain what is meant by inference.", "List three steps for summarising a passage."])]),

